>> 
>> You don't engrave ON it, you engrave WITH it.  When it asks you to 
>> what you want to engrave with, instead of saying "-" (fingers), give
>> the letter of your weapon.  When you engrave one (and only one) 
>> letter with a weapon, the weapon dulls by losing one enchantment.
>> You can then read another blessed scroll of enchant weapon to try
>> again to get it to +7.
>> 
>> Explictly, where "a" is my +X long sword and Z is any letter, type "EaZ".
>> You now have a +(X-1) long sword.  This will work with any edged weapon.
>> The only important weapon which is not edged (that I can think of) is
>> Mjollnir.  So engaving with Mjollnir won't dull it.  If you really, really
>> want to dull Mjollnir, read a cursed scroll of enchant weapon.
>> -- 
>> Peter Snelling
>
>I must be missing something really badly here. What is the point of engraving
>with a weapon?

I previously gave a spoiler for getting your weapon up to +7.  If you read
enough enchant weapon scrolls to get it to +5, and then read one more 
blessed scroll of enchant weapon, it may go to +7.  In which case, great.

Sometimes, however, when you read a blessed scroll of enchant weapon on 
a +5 weapon it only goes up to +6.  This is a problem, because it is risky
to enchant when it's +6 or more (the weapon might blow up).  So, if you 
only get it to +6, engrave with it (as above) to dull it to +5, and then 
try another blessed scroll of enchant weapon.
